  basalt.poolController -[model]-> basalt.poolRuntime "inference on port 8317"
  basalt.poolController -[sync]-> basalt.poolRuntime "management on port 8327" {
    description "Home completes OAuth by atomically storing committed credential UUIDs in the existing session JSONB. Its v8 status returns those exact IDs, including the first successful plugin poll; pending secrets are cleared and the completion record keeps its one-minute lifetime. The acquiring host refuses success without those IDs. Pool views overlap membership and credential reads, then read only the membership-scoped quota details with bounded concurrency. Operation timings record fixed names, durations and counts without Home response data; readiness warming is timed separately. Subscription checks and idle sampling share a persisted 30-minute forced-collection cap per account. Checks allow the owner or current pool audience and verify exact credential membership. Separate owner-only observation read and refresh routes return a bounded projection, preserve quota alongside credential failures, and bypass OAuth attribution state. A check's Home reads share a twelve-second deadline, including membership and credentials, and wait at most nine seconds for a completed collection. Current pool members can force capped quota collection. Owner-only pool app tools pause and resume routing and clear quota cooldowns. Banked-reset reads retain provider credit IDs, expiry and model provider use conditions. Reset redemption uses fixed provider URLs through Home token substitution; per-account queues and durable request receipts preserve uncertain outcomes and retry follow-up work without repeating a confirmed spend."
  }
  basalt.poolController -[sync]-> basalt.poolRuntime "start or reuse; GET /ready on port 8318" {
    description "The controller persists the desired launch revision after stopping an older boot and before starting its replacement. A readiness timeout or controller replacement preserves a running boot for that revision; a changed image or secret revision still replaces it. Readiness remains a separate bounded check. A failed revision write prevents launch, and a failed stop leaves the old revision recorded."
  }
